DISKO 2 — Detailed Walkthrough
Platform: PicoCTF · Category: Forensics · Difficulty: Medium · Date: 2026-09-17

1. Understanding the Challenge
The challenge provides a raw disk image (disko-2.dd.gz) containing multiple partitions. The hint says:

"The right one is Linux! One wrong step and it's all gone!"

This means:

There are multiple partitions with decoy content
Only the Linux partition contains the real flag
"One wrong step" = if you extract the wrong partition (FAT32), you'll find decoys or nothing useful
2. Partition Layout (from mmls)
Slot    Start       End         Length      Description
000:    0           0           1           Primary Table
001:    0           2047        2048        Unallocated (MBR gap)
002:    2048        53247       51200       Linux (0x83)        ← TARGET
003:    53248       118783      65536       Win95 FAT32 (0x0b)  ← DECOY
004:    118784      204799      86016       Unallocated

Key values for dd:

skip=2048 → start sector of Linux partition
count=51200 → number of sectors in Linux partition
bs=512 → standard sector size
3. Why dd with skip/count?
The disk image is a raw binary — no filesystem abstraction. To isolate a partition:

┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  disko-2.dd (full disk)                                     │
│                                                             │
│  [MBR 512B][gap][ Linux Partition ][ FAT32 Partition ][gap] │
│   offset 0   2048   sectors 2048-53247   53248-118783       │
│                                                             │
│  dd skip=2048 count=51200 → extracts ONLY the Linux part   │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

Parameter	Meaning	Value
bs=512	Block size (bytes)	512 (standard sector)
skip=2048	Skip N blocks from start	2048 (partition start)
count=51200	Read N blocks	51200 (partition size)

Output: 51200 × 512 = 26,214,400 bytes = 25 MiB

4. Finding the Flag
strings linux.img | grep picoCTF

How it works:

strings extracts all sequences of ≥4 printable ASCII characters from the binary
grep picoCTF filters for the flag pattern
Result: exactly one match → picoCTF{4_P4Rt_1t_i5_90a3f3d1}
No decoys in this case (unlike some similar challenges with ~100 fake flags).

5. Alternative Methods (for harder variants)
If strings returns multiple flags or nothing:

# Mount the partition (needs root)
sudo mkdir /mnt/disko
sudo mount -o loop,ro linux.img /mnt/disko
find /mnt/disko -type f -exec grep -l "picoCTF" {} \;

# Check deleted files (ext filesystem)
fls -r -p -o 2048 disko-2.dd | grep -i "del\|pico"

# Read deleted file by inode
icat -o 2048 disko-2.dd <inode>

# Check unallocated space (carving)
foremost -t all -i linux.img -o output/

6. Flag
picoCTF{4_P4Rt_1t_i5_90a3f3d1}

The name 4_P4Rt_1t is a play on "for partition" — reinforcing the core skill tested.

7. Key Concepts Tested
Concept	Detail
MBR / Partition Table	First 512 bytes of disk; describes partition layout
mmls	Sleuth Kit tool to read partition tables from raw images
dd with skip/count	Binary-level partition extraction without mounting
strings	Extract readable text from binary data
Filesystem types	0x83 = Linux ext, 0x0b = FAT32
Offset calculation	Partition start sector × 512 = byte offset

8. Quick Reference (Cheat Sheet)
# Full pipeline
gunzip disko-2.dd.gz
mmls disko-2.dd                          # identify partitions
dd if=disko-2.dd of=linux.img bs=512 skip=2048 count=51200
strings linux.img | grep picoCTF         # extract flag

